I am not arguing for necessities, I am arguing for ways to improve the 
game.  For me being less at the mercy of the rng and being able to rely 
more on your own skills is an improvement.

> Marvin's 12 tournament ascensions in a row are
> highly unlikely to include no games with resource
> scarcities.

He is a very skillful player, so what?  I still bet he had no extremely 
unlucky combination of resource scarcities.

> I just don't see that NetHack has to guarantee
> "every game is ascendable" to players who lack the
> skills to get around resource shortages. It seems
> already to guarantee "every game is ascendable if
> you're good enough".

Nonsense.  There are games that you lose by bad luck and no skill can 
prevent this.  (Being hit by a poisoned arrow from an orc turning round 
the corner on Dlvl 3, with no source of poison resistance available 
prior to the event; being killed by a rolling boulder trap on Dlvl 2; 
many other examples are available.)  I would be all in favor of 
eliminating these inevitable deaths, but I don't see any way.  I don't 
however care too much about early inevitable deaths.  On the other hand, 
I think that deaths late in the game should not be inevitable but depend 
in frequency on whether you are using your skills to avoid them.  To 
keep up the tension you may avoid that the "residual" death probability 
becomes zero (which was essentially the case back at the time of 
NH3.1.3, where making no mistake after having reached certain goals 
meant you would ascend with probability one), but it should be very 
small.  Otherwise, I would not consider the game worth playing.

Every game is *in principle* ascendable, but that's true not only for 
good players.  You can win by good luck.  You can get very far without 
even knowing about many dangers (see the example of Ellora).
